WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:05.090
At its core, American Dad is a family sitcom…
crossed with the craziest elements of spy

00:00:05.090 --> 00:00:06.740
movies and science fiction.

00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:11.320
More than 200 episodes of the series have
aired, centered around CIA agent Stan Smith,

00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:15.249
his family, their live-in alien Roger, and
a talking fish named Klaus.

00:00:15.249 --> 00:00:17.830
Here's a look behind the scenes of American
Dad!

00:00:17.830 --> 00:00:19.590
The evolution of Klaus

00:00:19.590 --> 00:00:24.350
American Dad! is almost definitely the only
show on TV to have a goldfish who speaks with

00:00:24.350 --> 00:00:25.740
a German accent.

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:30.570
That's Klaus, a once-human German ski jumper
whose brain was switched a goldfish by cutting-edge

00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:34.700
CIA technology — and who now lives in a
bowl at the Smith residence.

00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:36.520
"Hour 36.

00:00:36.740 --> 00:00:38.680
The sun seems like it comes every day now.

00:00:41.380 --> 00:00:42.920
The cat's back!"

00:00:42.930 --> 00:00:46.219
Not only was Klaus once human, he was also
once French.

00:00:46.219 --> 00:00:48.050
Series co-creator Seth MacFarlane explained:

00:00:48.050 --> 00:00:52.559
"Dee Baker… the character of Klaus was originally
supposed to be French."

00:00:52.559 --> 00:00:59.309
"I remembered that Dee had come in to do some
animal sounds on Family Guy and had kinda

00:00:59.309 --> 00:01:02.819
warmed himself up in the booth by doing this
German accent."

00:01:02.819 --> 00:01:06.409
MacFarlane fell in love with Baker's alternate
take on the fish, and Baker got the part,

00:01:06.409 --> 00:01:09.859
beating out Billy West and John DiMaggio of
Futurama.

00:01:09.859 --> 00:01:11.229
They dropped the politics

00:01:11.229 --> 00:01:16.299
A show about a very conservative CIA agent
who lives and works in the Washington, D.C.

00:01:16.299 --> 00:01:20.479
area is bound to be at least a little political,
but the American Dad! writers have toned down

00:01:20.479 --> 00:01:22.380
that kind of content over the years.

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:26.539
It's not because the show's networks, Fox
or TBS, told them to, or that they were afraid

00:01:26.539 --> 00:01:27.799
of offending viewers.

00:01:27.799 --> 00:01:30.450
They found that political references dated
the show.

00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:35.119
For example, in 2005, President George W.
Bush nominated White House counsel Harriet

00:01:35.119 --> 00:01:36.520
Miers for the Supreme Court.

00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:37.520
American Dad!

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:40.359
Wrote in a joke about her, but Bush withdrew
the nomination.

00:01:40.359 --> 00:01:43.759
A few months later, the episode with the Miers
joke aired.

00:01:43.759 --> 00:01:45.670
Co-creator Mike Barker told The AV Club,

00:01:45.670 --> 00:01:49.259
"I remember watching on air and having to
Google who our own joke was, because it had

00:01:49.259 --> 00:01:51.509
been so long since the joke was pitched."

00:01:51.509 --> 00:01:56.700
In the end, American Dad moved away from politics
to remain more relevant in reruns.

00:01:56.700 --> 00:01:58.109
Going too far

00:01:58.109 --> 00:02:02.080
American Dad! can push the envelope in terms
of content, but there have been only a few

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:04.300
times cast or crew drew a line.

00:02:04.300 --> 00:02:08.690
Patrick Stewart, who plays CIA director Avery
Bullock, didn't want to deliver a joke that

00:02:08.690 --> 00:02:14.540
strongly suggested his character was a creep,
according to a 2012 San Diego Comic Con panel.

00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:19.200
Even though Stewart had no qualms earlier
about singing a suggestive Oingo Boingo song.

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:23.230
"I, I love girls, they make me feel so good.

00:02:23.230 --> 00:02:28.310
I, I love girls, they make me feel so bad."

00:02:28.310 --> 00:02:32.670
Another instance of self-editing personally
involved co-creator Seth MacFarlane.

00:02:32.670 --> 00:02:36.799
There was a reference in a script to a character
having lost a lot of weight, or, as the original

00:02:36.799 --> 00:02:39.180
script called it, "doing a reverse Jason Segel."

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:42.440
MacFarlane, who's friends with Segel, asked
for a rewrite.

00:02:42.440 --> 00:02:46.390
Writers gave Steve's voice actor, Scott Grimes,
the option to say "Russell Crowe," which then

00:02:46.390 --> 00:02:47.580
made Grimes uncomfortable.

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:51.790
They ultimately decided on "Vince Vaughn,"
but still recorded him saying Russell Crowe,

00:02:51.790 --> 00:02:53.760
"in case it was funnier."

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:55.220
Pulled from the schedule

00:02:55.220 --> 00:02:59.250
It's not just a few lines that have had to
be cut from the script — at least twice,

00:02:59.250 --> 00:03:03.290
episodes were significantly delayed so they
wouldn't seem to make light of national tragedies.

00:03:03.290 --> 00:03:07.870
In April 2011, Fox planned a night of crossover
episodes where a hurricane would impact the

00:03:07.870 --> 00:03:13.040
lives of characters on all three of Seth MacFarlane's
animated shows, American Dad!, Family Guy,

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:14.250
and The Cleveland Show.

00:03:14.250 --> 00:03:18.370
In the weeks leading up to that event, storms
and tornadoes ravaged the southeastern United

00:03:18.370 --> 00:03:21.980
States, and Fox pulled the hurricane-themed
episodes, pushing "Night of the Hurricane"

00:03:21.980 --> 00:03:23.910
to October 2011.

00:03:23.910 --> 00:03:27.900
The following year, the American Dad! episode
"Minstrel Krampus" was scheduled to air on

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:29.189
December 16th.

00:03:29.189 --> 00:03:33.790
The Christmas-themed episode included an extremely
violent scene in which Santa and Stan Smith

00:03:33.790 --> 00:03:37.990
storm the castle where the mythical creature
Krampus lives, violently killing Beauty and

00:03:37.990 --> 00:03:40.150
the Beast-styled animated objects.

00:03:40.150 --> 00:03:44.000
Just before it aired, Fox removed "Minstrel
Krampus" from its schedule in light of that

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:47.900
week's tragic school shooting at Sandy Hook
Elementary School in Connecticut.

00:03:47.900 --> 00:03:50.920
The episode ultimately aired a year later.

00:03:50.920 --> 00:03:52.379
The musical cast

00:03:52.379 --> 00:03:56.579
American Dad! employs some top-notch vocal
talent, and some of its cast members have

00:03:56.579 --> 00:04:00.310
such skilled and varied vocal abilities that
they've released albums.

00:04:00.310 --> 00:04:05.170
In 2012, Rachael MacFarlane, who voices Hayley
Smith, released an album called Hayley Sings,

00:04:05.170 --> 00:04:09.219
a collection of sultry nightclub jazz; the
American Dad! episode "Love, A.D.

00:04:09.219 --> 00:04:10.219
Style."

00:04:10.219 --> 00:04:13.560
in which Hayley becomes a nightclub singer,
features a couple of songs from the album.

00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:17.470
Scott Grimes, whose vocal talents are also
often used in the show, has released three

00:04:17.470 --> 00:04:23.580
albums: a self-titled pop record in 1990,
Livin' on the Run in 2005, and Drive in 2010.

00:04:23.580 --> 00:04:27.410
And singing traditional pop standards with
a big band is just one of Seth MacFarlane's

00:04:27.410 --> 00:04:28.410
many jobs.

00:04:28.410 --> 00:04:32.550
He's made three albums of the stuff: Music
is Better Than Words, Holiday for Swing, and

00:04:32.550 --> 00:04:37.330
No One Ever Tells You — which actually hit
number one on Billboard's jazz albums chart.

00:04:37.330 --> 00:04:38.330
Have a great night

00:04:38.330 --> 00:04:40.750
"Bye, have a beautiful time!"

00:04:40.750 --> 00:04:45.550
Underdog Productions' end card for every episode
of American Dad! uses a video of a smiling

00:04:45.550 --> 00:04:49.050
man giving a wave and a thumbs-up, usually
wishing everyone a good night.

00:04:49.050 --> 00:04:51.010
"Bye, have a great time!"

00:04:51.010 --> 00:04:55.140
According to the show's DVD commentaries,
he's a guy identified only as Security Officer

00:04:55.140 --> 00:04:58.650
Peña who worked at the building where Underdog
Productions makes the show.

00:04:58.650 --> 00:05:03.139
Peña no longer works there, but his presence
remains a fixture at the end of each episode…

00:05:03.139 --> 00:05:04.139
even when he's not there.

00:05:04.139 --> 00:05:05.490
"Come back, you cowards, I'll kill you!

00:05:05.490 --> 00:05:09.280
I'll hunt you down and kill you!

00:05:11.260 --> 00:05:12.020
Bye!

00:05:12.020 --> 00:05:13.440
Have a beautiful time!"

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:14.759
Thanks for watching!

00:05:14.759 --> 00:05:17.120
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:19.140
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

